Title: a FOREACH with no body returns THE LIST in BricsCAD and NIL in AutoCAD
Status: RESOLVED 2026-09-11 -- dialect-discriminated with a portability
        warning (clautolisp 2.2.4 / alfe 2.2.5). See RESOLUTION below.
Found: 2026-08-31, by the foreach-scope probe suite, on both platforms

THE MEASUREMENT

    (foreach e (list 1 2 3))          ; no body at all

    AutoCAD 2026      nil
    BricsCAD V25/V26  (1 2 3)         ; macOS AND Windows, same answer
    clautolisp        nil

Every other case in the suite agrees across all three engines -- the
binding rule, the value of a normal FOREACH, the value over an empty
list. This one does not.

WHY IT LOOKS LIKE BricsCAD RETURNS THE LIST

FOREACH is specified to yield its last body value. With NO body there
is no such value, so AutoCAD's nil is the reading that follows the
words. BricsCAD appears to fall back on the value of the LIST argument
-- which is what a loop written as `evaluate the list, then iterate'
would leave in an accumulator if the body never wrote one. That is a
plausible implementation accident rather than a designed answer, but it
is CONSISTENT: both platforms, both versions probed, same result.

WHAT CLAUTOLISP DOES, AND WHY

NIL, following AutoCAD and the specification's wording. Chosen rather
than deferred because the alternative -- returning the list -- would
mean adopting an undocumented quirk of one vendor as the default
behaviour of a dialect-neutral implementation, which is backwards.

NOT ELEVATED TO THE NORMATIVE SPEC either way: this is vendor
divergence, and the spec records what is common. The BricsCAD answer
belongs in the *** clautolisp / vendor-divergence section of the
FOREACH page, per the convention.

RESOLUTION (pjb, 2026-09-11): follow BricsCAD under --dialect bricscad,
and DISCRIMINATE BY DIALECT WITH WARNINGS. pjb confirmed the reference
value at three consoles -- clautolisp, AutoCAD 2022 (Windows) and
BricsCAD V26 (macOS) / V25 (Windows) -- and stated the spec: FOREACH
"returns the final body value or nil if no body exists". So nil is
correct and BricsCAD is the deviant, and the divergence "must be
documented and discriminated by dialect with warnings".

Implemented exactly like every other :vendor-divergence
(%RESOLVED-DIVERGENCE-POLICY in cador is the template):

  dialect                      body-less FOREACH over a non-empty list
  ---------------------------  ---------------------------------------
  --autocad / --clautolisp     nil     (spec value, silent)
  --strict                     nil     (spec value, + portability warning)
  --dialect bricscad           the list (BricsCAD's value, + warning)
  --lax                        the list (silent)

An empty list is nil under every dialect (the hosts agree), so it does
not diverge and does not warn.

The decision lives in ONE place, FOREACH-EMPTY-BODY-RESULT
(clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp), which the interpreter
(EVAL-FOREACH-FORM) and the compiler (transpile.lisp, open-coded FOREACH)
both call -- so the two forks cannot answer differently. The warning is
registered as `foreach-empty-body' in portability.lisp and appears in the
generated dialect-warnings annex. Documented in the FOREACH page's
*** clautolisp section. Tested: FOREACH-EMPTY-BODY-IS-DIALECT-DISCRIMINATED
(runtime) and COMPILED-FOREACH-EMPTY-BODY-HONOURS-THE-DIALECT (compiler).

HOW NEARLY IT WAS NEVER FOUND

This case cost two days and three wrong guesses, and none of them was
about the answer. `(foreach e lst)' with no body, written directly in
the probe file, made BricsCAD refuse to LOAD THE FILE -- silently,
before any probe ran, taking the other cases with it. AutoCAD and
clautolisp both accept the form, so nothing local showed it. The suite
only produced this row once the form was held as TEXT and READ at run
time.

So BricsCAD executes the form perfectly well; it will not READ it in a
file it is loading. That distinction is itself worth a ticket --
issues/open/bricscad-refuses-to-load-what-it-will-run.issue -- and it
is the reason the probe harness now has PROBE_SPLIT_SUITES.
